- Story & Context: According to shoreline myth, the fogged reefs off Kingston Spit are still patrolled by Captain Kroll, who supposedly steers ghost freighters through the shallows playing a nine-foot contrabass flute carved from an engine-room cooling pipe. Local beachcombers assemble on the gravel banks at winter twilight, lighting fires of diesel-soaked hemp rope and shaking rusted cans of ball-bearings to keep the sluggish dancehall tempo alive while waiting for the deep, oceanic horns to answer from the dark water. During last year's freezing solstice, fifty onlookers watched three pilotless cargo hulls reverse away from the jagged rocks in perfect synchronized time, their horns booming across the surf as a windblown voice sang through the spindrift: "Woven in the brine, iron never weeps twice."
